(function(){window.PhysicaRankPyqNeet_physics_systemofparticlesandrotationalmotion=[{id:"neet-2026r-q006",pn:6,y:"Re-NEET 2026 \xB7 21 Jun",tp:"System of Particles and Rotational Motion",st:"Linear momentum of a system of particles",q:"A frictionless circular wire of unit radius is fixed on the horizontal plane. Two-point particles of unit mass start moving simultaneously from point A (\u03B8 = \u03C0/2) with identical uniform angular speeds in opposite directions, and meet again at point B (\u03B8 = \u2013\u03C0/2). During this time, which of the following figures schematically represent the magnitude of the total linear momentum P of the system, as a function of \u03B8?",o:["Graph 1: a curve like |cos \u03B8| with two downward cusps: P starts at a maximum at the left, falls to 0 at the first cusp, rises to a smaller peak and falls to 0 at the second cusp","Graph 2: a single smooth semicircular arch starting at 0 at \u03B8 = \u03C0/2 and returning to 0 at \u03B8 = \u2013\u03C0/2","Graph 3: a straight line falling from a maximum at \u03B8 = \u03C0/2 to 0 at \u03B8 = \u2013\u03C0/2","Graph 4: a horizontal constant line"],c:1,s:`Both particles have the same speed v, moving in opposite directions, at angle \u03B8 from the horizontal.
Net momentum: P_net = \u2013mv sin\u03B8 \xEE \u2013 mv cos\u03B8 \u0135 + mv sin\u03B8 \xEE \u2013 mv cos\u03B8 \u0135.
P_net = \u20132mv cos\u03B8 \u0135.
At \u03B8 = \u03C0/2, P_net = 0.
At \u03B8 = \u2013\u03C0/2, P_net = 0.
So P is zero at both ends and largest in the middle, like cos \u03B8.
The most suitable graph is a single smooth arch.
Note: Option graphs are drawn pictures; the option texts in o are my descriptions, see dia. Printed answer (2) = single arch from 0 to 0`,fig:{w:350,h:345,alt:"Circle with points A (top) and B (bottom), angle \u03B8 from the horizontal, two particles leaving A in opposite directions, and four P versus \u03B8 graphs (1) to (4).",d:[["c",80,60,30],["l",80,24,80,96,"d"],["l",35,60,125,60,"d"],["t",70,18,"A","e"],["t",70,110,"B","e"],["p","M72 25C62 28 54 36 50 46","a"],["p","M88 25C98 28 106 36 110 46","a"],["p","M92 60A12 12 0 0 0 87 50"],["t",96,56,"\u03B8",""],["l",45,210,45,150,"a"],["l",45,210,135,210,"a"],["t",43,148,"P","e"],["t",137,214,"\u03B8",""],["t",45,224,"\u03C0/2","m"],["t",99,224,"\u2013\u03C0/2","m"],["t",3,184,"(1)",""],["l",235,210,235,150,"a"],["l",235,210,325,210,"a"],["t",233,148,"P","e"],["t",327,214,"\u03B8",""],["t",235,224,"\u03C0/2","m"],["t",289,224,"\u2013\u03C0/2","m"],["t",193,184,"(2)",""],["l",45,310,45,250,"a"],["l",45,310,135,310,"a"],["t",43,248,"P","e"],["t",137,314,"\u03B8",""],["t",45,324,"\u03C0/2","m"],["t",99,324,"\u2013\u03C0/2","m"],["t",3,284,"(3)",""],["l",235,310,235,250,"a"],["l",235,310,325,310,"a"],["t",233,248,"P","e"],["t",327,314,"\u03B8",""],["t",235,324,"\u03C0/2","m"],["t",289,324,"\u2013\u03C0/2","m"],["t",193,284,"(4)",""],["p","M45 154C45 180 60 195 70 210"],["p","M70 210C75 190 90 175 99 175"],["p","M235 210C235 160 289 160 289 210"],["l",45,254,99,310,""],["l",235,270,315,270,""]]},sfig:{w:150,h:115,alt:"P versus \u03B8: a single semicircular arch from zero at \u03C0/2 to zero at \u2013\u03C0/2.",d:[["l",20,90,20,10,"a"],["l",20,90,120,90,"a"],["t",18,10,"P","e"],["t",124,94,"\u03B8",""],["t",20,106,"\u03C0/2","m"],["t",88,106,"\u2013\u03C0/2","m"],["p","M20 90C20 25 90 25 90 90"]]}},{id:"neet-2026-q020",pn:20,y:"NEET 2026 \xB7 3 May",tp:"System of Particles and Rotational Motion",st:"Moment of inertia",q:"A thin wire of length L and linear mass density m is bent into a circular ring (in x\u2013y plane) with centre C as shown in figure. The moment of inertia of the ring about an axis yy\u2032 will be:",o:["3mL\xB3/(8\u03C0\xB2)","3mL\xB3/(8\u03C0)","3mL\xB2/(8\u03C0\xB2)","3mL\xB2/(8\u03C0)"],c:0,s:`Mass of the wire = (linear mass density) \xD7 (length), so M = mL.
L = 2\u03C0r, so r = L/(2\u03C0).
The axis yy\u2032 is tangent to the ring, so use the parallel axis theorem.
I_yy\u2032 = I_CM + Mr\xB2 = Mr\xB2/2 + Mr\xB2 = (3/2)Mr\xB2.
I_yy\u2032 = (3/2) \xD7 (mL) \xD7 (L/(2\u03C0))\xB2 = 3mL\xB3/(8\u03C0\xB2).`,fig:{w:190,h:150,alt:"A circular ring with centre C in the x-y plane; the vertical line yy\u2032 touches the ring at its rightmost point, and the x axis points right from that point",d:[["c",60,75,48],["c",60,75,2,"F"],["l",108,12,108,138],["t",112,14,"y\u2032"],["t",112,142,"y"],["l",60,75,108,75,"d"],["l",112,75,152,75,"a"],["t",140,92,"x"],["t",54,93,"C"]]}},{id:"neet-2026r-q027",pn:27,y:"Re-NEET 2026 \xB7 21 Jun",tp:"System of Particles and Rotational Motion",st:"Parallel axis theorem",q:"A solid sphere A of radius R and mass M is attached at a point to a smaller solid sphere B of radius r < R and mass m < M. Assume that the line joining their centres lies along the horizontal. The moment of inertia of the system calculated about a vertical axis passing through the centre of A is I_A and that calculated about a vertical axis passing through the centre of B is I_B. The difference I_A \u2212 I_B is:",o:["(m \u2212 M)(R + r)\xB2","(m \u2212 M)(R \u2212 r)\xB2","0","(M \u2212 m)(R + r)\xB2"],c:0,s:`Use I_C = (2/5)mR\xB2 for a solid sphere about its own centre, and the parallel axis theorem.
About the axis through the centre of A: I_A = (2/5)MR\xB2 + (2/5)mr\xB2 + m(R + r)\xB2 ...(1)
About the axis through the centre of B: I_B = (2/5)MR\xB2 + M(R + r)\xB2 + (2/5)mr\xB2 ...(2)
Subtracting (1) and (2): I_A \u2212 I_B = (m \u2212 M)(R + r)\xB2.`,fig:{w:330,h:155,alt:"Two views of a large sphere A touching a smaller sphere B on its right, a dotted line joining the centres; the vertical dashed axis passes through the centre of A and is labelled I_A in the left view, and through the centre of B and is labelled I_B in the right view.",d:[["c",70,80,40],["c",122,80,12],["l",70,80,122,80,"d"],["l",70,15,70,145,"d"],["t",32,52,"A","e"],["t",138,56,"B"],["t",75,22,"I_A"],["c",230,80,40],["c",282,80,12],["l",230,80,282,80,"d"],["l",282,15,282,145,"d"],["t",192,52,"A","e"],["t",298,56,"B"],["t",287,22,"I_B"]]},sfig:{w:330,h:180,alt:"Big sphere M of radius R and small sphere m of radius r touching it; in (1) the vertical dashed axis I_A passes through the centre of M, in (2) the axis I_B passes through the centre of m.",d:[["c",70,80,40],["c",122,80,12],["l",70,80,122,80,"d"],["c",70,80,2,"F"],["l",70,15,70,145,"d"],["t",40,48,"M","e"],["t",138,62,"m"],["t",84,74,"R","s"],["t",118,76,"r","s"],["t",75,22,"I_A"],["t",90,168,"(1)","m"],["c",230,80,40],["c",282,80,12],["l",230,80,282,80,"d"],["c",230,80,2,"F"],["l",282,15,282,145,"d"],["t",200,48,"M","e"],["t",298,62,"m"],["t",244,74,"R","s"],["t",278,76,"r","s"],["t",287,22,"I_B"],["t",250,168,"(2)","m"]]}},{id:"neet-2026-q038",pn:38,y:"NEET 2026 \xB7 3 May",tp:"System of Particles and Rotational Motion",st:"Rotational kinematics",q:"The angular speed of a flywheel is increased from 600 rpm to 1200 rpm in 10 s. The number of revolutions completed by the flywheel during this time is:",o:["600","900","300","150"],c:3,s:`\u03B1 = (\u03C9\u2082 \u2212 \u03C9\u2081)/\u0394t = ((1200 \u2212 600)/10) \xD7 (2\u03C0/60) = 2\u03C0 rad/s\xB2.
\u03C9\u2082 = 1200 \xD7 2\u03C0/60 = 40\u03C0 and \u03C9\u2081 = 600 \xD7 2\u03C0/60 = 20\u03C0.
Use \u03C9\u2082\xB2 = \u03C9\u2081\xB2 + 2\u03B1\u03B8: (40\u03C0)\xB2 = (20\u03C0)\xB2 + 2 \xD7 2\u03C0 \xD7 \u03B8.
1200\u03C0\xB2 = 4\u03C0\u03B8, so \u03B8 = 300\u03C0 radian.
Number of revolutions = \u03B8/2\u03C0 = 150.`},{id:"neet-2026r-q045",pn:45,y:"Re-NEET 2026 \xB7 21 Jun",tp:"System of Particles and Rotational Motion",st:"Angular momentum of rigid body",q:"A thin horizontal disc is rotating about a vertical axis passing through its fixed centre O. Its angular momentum is LA and LB computed about points A and B, respectively, with OB = 2 \xD7 OA. The value of LA/LB is:",o:["1/2","1","2","1/4"],c:1,s:`Angular momentum: L = r \xD7 P + Icm \u03C9.
The disc rotates about its fixed centre, so the momentum P of the centre of mass is 0 for both points.
So LA = LB = Icm \u03C9.
LA/LB = 1.`,fig:{w:300,h:170,alt:"A disc with centre O and a rotation arrow near its upper left; along a dotted line from O, point A lies on the rim and point B lies outside, further right.",d:[["c",90,85,60],["c",90,85,2,"F"],["t",86,112,"O","s"],["l",90,85,150,85,"d"],["c",150,85,2,"F"],["t",147,112,"A","s"],["l",150,85,230,85,"d"],["c",230,85,2,"F"],["t",228,112,"B","s"],["p","M68 52 Q56 60 52 72 M52 72 L52 62 M52 72 L62 70"]]}},{id:"neet-2025-q017",pn:17,y:"NEET 2025",tp:"System of Particles and Rotational Motion",st:"Conservation of angular momentum",q:"The Sun rotates around its centre once in 27 days. What will be the period of revolution if the Sun were to expand to twice its present radius without any external influence? Assume the Sun to be a sphere of uniform density.",o:["100 days","105 days","115 days","108 days"],c:3,s:`Taking the Sun as a solid sphere, I = (2/5)mR\xB2.
By conservation of angular momentum, I\u2032\u03C9\u2032 = I\u03C9
(2/5)m(2R)\xB2 \xD7 (2\u03C0/T\u2032) = (2/5)mR\xB2 \xD7 (2\u03C0/T)
T\u2032 = 4T = 4 \xD7 27 = 108 days`},{id:"neet-2025-q022",pn:22,y:"NEET 2025",tp:"System of Particles and Rotational Motion",st:"Equilibrium of a rod",q:"A uniform rod of mass 20 kg and length 5 m leans against a smooth vertical wall making an angle of 60\xB0 with it. The other end rests on a rough horizontal floor. The friction force that the floor exerts on the rod is (Take g = 10 m/s\xB2)",o:["100 N","100\u221A3 N","200 N","200\u221A3 N"],c:1,s:`For translational equilibrium:
N\u2081 = Mg
N\u2082 = f
For rotational equilibrium, torque about A (the foot of the rod):
Mg (L/2) cos \u03B8 = N\u2082 L sin \u03B8
(Mg/2) cot \u03B8 = N\u2082 = f
(Mg/2) cot 30\xB0 = f
(Mg/2)\u221A3 = N\u2082
100\u221A3 = f`,sfig:{w:290,h:215,alt:"Rod AB leans on a smooth wall at B with its foot A on a rough floor; forces N1, N2, f and Mg are marked with the 60 and 30 degree angles",d:[["l",63,22,63,142],["l",62,142,240,142],["l",62,147,240,147,"d"],["l",63,43,160,142,"B"],["t",44,14,"Smooth"],["t",47,47,"B"],["l",63,43,112,43,"a"],["t",116,47,"N\u2082"],["t",68,100,"60\xB0","s"],["t",128,84,"L"],["l",162,130,162,102,"a"],["t",167,98,"N\u2081"],["t",116,139,"f, 30\xB0","s"],["l",160,157,112,157,"b"],["t",168,162,"A"],["c",111,92,2.5,"F"],["l",111,92,111,190,"a"],["t",100,206,"Mg"],["t",116,178,"L/2"],["t",136,182,"cos\u03B8"],["l",160,157,160,157],["t",14,108,"L sin\u03B8"],["l",56,106,66,106],["l",222,152,222,168,"a"],["t",196,182,"Rough surface"]]}},{id:"neet-2025-q039",pn:39,y:"NEET 2025",tp:"System of Particles and Rotational Motion",st:"Moment of inertia, parallel axis theorem",q:"A sphere of radius R is cut from a larger solid sphere of radius 2R as shown in the figure. The ratio of the moment of inertia of the smaller sphere to that of the rest part of the sphere about the Y-axis is:",o:["7/8","7/40","7/57","7/64"],c:2,s:`For the larger solid sphere about the diameter (Y-axis): I_whole = (2/5)M(2R)\xB2 = (8/5)MR\xB2.
Density is uniform, so M/V_whole = M\u2032/V_smaller.
M/[(4/3)\u03C0(2R)\xB3] = M\u2032/[(4/3)\u03C0R\xB3], so M\u2032 = M/8.
Use the parallel axis theorem for the smaller sphere (its centre is at distance R from the Y-axis):
I\u2032 = I_cm + M\u2032R\xB2 = (2/5)(MR\xB2/8) + MR\xB2/8 = (7/40)MR\xB2.
Ratio = I_smaller/I_remaining = I\u2032/(I_whole \u2212 I\u2032).
= (7/40)MR\xB2 / [(8/5 \u2212 7/40)MR\xB2] = 7/(64 \u2212 7) = 7/57.`,fig:{w:250,h:236,alt:"Large sphere of radius 2R with a sphere of radius R cut out on the right, touching the Y-axis, rest hatched; 2R marked along X",d:[["l",89.8,47.2,151.8,47.2],["l",77.9,53.6,163.7,53.6],["l",69.4,60,172.2,60],["l",62.8,66.4,178.8,66.4],["l",57.6,72.8,184,72.8],["l",53.4,79.2,188.2,79.2],["l",50,85.6,139.8,85.6],["l",177,85.6,191.6,85.6],["l",47.3,92,131.3,92],["l",185.5,92,194.3,92],["l",45.2,98.4,126.1,98.4],["l",190.7,98.4,196.4,98.4],["l",43.7,104.8,122.8,104.8],["l",194,104.8,197.9,104.8],["l",42.8,111.2,120.8,111.2],["l",196,111.2,198.8,111.2],["l",42.4,117.6,120,117.6],["l",196.8,117.6,199.2,117.6],["l",42.5,124,120.3,124],["l",196.5,124,199.1,124],["l",43.2,130.4,121.7,130.4],["l",195.1,130.4,198.4,130.4],["l",44.4,136.8,124.3,136.8],["l",192.5,136.8,197.2,136.8],["l",46.2,143.2,128.4,143.2],["l",188.4,143.2,195.4,143.2],["l",48.5,149.6,134.9,149.6],["l",181.9,149.6,193.1,149.6],["l",51.6,156,147.4,156],["l",169.4,156,190,156],["l",55.4,162.4,186.2,162.4],["l",60.1,168.8,181.5,168.8],["l",65.9,175.2,175.7,175.2],["l",73.3,181.6,168.3,181.6],["l",83.2,188,158.4,188],["l",98.6,194.4,143,194.4],["c",120.8,119.2,78.4],["c",158.4,119.2,38.4],["l",6.4,119.2,232,119.2,"a"],["l",120.8,232,120.8,8,"a"],["t",237.6,123.2,"X"],["t",128.8,9.6,"Y"],["l",145.6,124,120.8,124,"a"],["l",171.2,124,196,124,"a"],["t",158.4,128,"2R","ms"]]},sfig:{w:256,h:260,alt:"Same figure with the centre of the small sphere marked at distance R from the Y-axis, a dotted line I cm through it and the axis I prime through the large sphere centre",d:[["l",92.6,53.6,155.4,53.6],["l",80.6,60,167.4,60],["l",72,66.4,176,66.4],["l",65.3,72.8,182.7,72.8],["l",60,79.2,188,79.2],["l",55.7,85.6,192.3,85.6],["l",52.2,92,144.2,92],["l",182.2,92,195.8,92],["l",49.4,98.4,135.4,98.4],["l",191,98.4,198.6,98.4],["l",47.2,104.8,130.1,104.8],["l",196.3,104.8,200.8,104.8],["l",45.6,111.2,126.5,111.2],["l",199.9,111.2,202.4,111.2],["l",44.6,117.6,124.4,117.6],["l",202,117.6,203.4,117.6],["l",44.1,124,123.3,124],["l",203.1,124,203.9,124],["l",44.1,130.4,123.3,130.4],["l",203.1,130.4,203.9,130.4],["l",44.6,136.8,124.4,136.8],["l",202,136.8,203.4,136.8],["l",45.6,143.2,126.5,143.2],["l",199.9,143.2,202.4,143.2],["l",47.2,149.6,130.1,149.6],["l",196.3,149.6,200.8,149.6],["l",49.4,156,135.4,156],["l",191,156,198.6,156],["l",52.2,162.4,144.2,162.4],["l",182.2,162.4,195.8,162.4],["l",55.7,168.8,192.3,168.8],["l",60,175.2,188,175.2],["l",65.3,181.6,182.7,181.6],["l",72,188,176,188],["l",80.6,194.4,167.4,194.4],["l",92.6,200.8,155.4,200.8],["c",124,127.2,80],["c",163.2,127.2,40],["l",4,127.2,240,127.2,"a"],["l",124,252,124,6.4,"a"],["l",163.2,22.4,163.2,240,"d"],["c",163.2,127.2,2.5,"F"],["t",249.6,130.4,"X"],["t",132.8,9.6,"Y"],["t",169.6,124,"R"],["t",158.4,248,"I","s"],["t",162.4,251.2,"cm","s"],["t",120.8,257.6,"I\u2032","s"]]}},{id:"neet-2024-q017",pn:17,y:"NEET 2024 \xB7 5 May",tp:"System of Particles and Rotational Motion",st:"Moment of inertia of rod",q:"The moment of inertia of a thin rod about an axis passing through its mid point and perpendicular to the rod is 2400 g cm\xB2. The length of the 400 g rod is nearly -",o:["20.7 cm","72.0 cm","8.5 cm","17.5 cm"],c:2,s:`For a thin rod about its midpoint, I = m\u2113\xB2/12.
2400 = 400 \xD7 \u2113\xB2/12.
\u2113\xB2 = 72.
\u2113 = \u221A72 = 8.48 cm, about 8.5 cm.`},{id:"neet-2024-q026",pn:26,y:"NEET 2024 \xB7 5 May",tp:"System of Particles and Rotational Motion",st:"Rolling motion",q:"A wheel of a bullock cart is rolling on a level road as shown in the figure below. If its linear speed is v in the direction shown, which one of the following options is correct (P and Q are any highest and lowest points on the wheel, respectively) ?",o:["Both the points P and Q move with equal speed","Point P has zero speed","Point P moves slower than point Q","Point P moves faster than point Q"],c:3,s:`In pure rolling, the top point has velocity 2v (forward).
The lowest point Q, touching the ground, has zero velocity.
So point P moves faster than point Q.`,fig:{w:190,h:125,alt:"A spoked wheel rolling to the right with linear speed v on level ground; P is the highest point and Q the lowest point touching the ground.",d:[["c",70,62,30],["l",40,62,100,62],["l",48.8,40.8,91.2,83.2],["l",70,32,70,92],["l",91.2,40.8,48.8,83.2],["l",30,92,112,92],["l",34,92,29,98],["l",39,92,34,98],["l",44,92,39,98],["l",49,92,44,98],["l",54,92,49,98],["l",59,92,54,98],["l",64,92,59,98],["l",69,92,64,98],["l",74,92,69,98],["l",79,92,74,98],["l",84,92,79,98],["l",89,92,84,98],["l",94,92,89,98],["l",99,92,94,98],["l",104,92,99,98],["l",109,92,104,98],["t",70,24,"P","m"],["t",70,118,"Q","m"],["l",108,62,142,62,"a"],["t",138,52,"v"]]},sfig:{w:190,h:105,alt:"Pure rolling: the top point P moves with velocity 2v, the centre with v, and the lowest point Q is at rest.",d:[["c",70,60,30],["l",30,90,112,90],["l",34,90,29,96],["l",39,90,34,96],["l",44,90,39,96],["l",49,90,44,96],["l",54,90,49,96],["l",59,90,54,96],["l",64,90,59,96],["l",69,90,64,96],["l",74,90,69,96],["l",79,90,74,96],["l",84,90,79,96],["l",89,90,84,96],["l",94,90,89,96],["l",99,90,94,96],["l",104,90,99,96],["l",109,90,104,96],["l",70,30,70,90],["l",70,30,118,30,"a"],["t",122,34,"P(2v)"],["l",70,60,100,60,"a"],["t",104,64,"v"],["t",74,84,"Q"]]}},{id:"neet-2023-q032",pn:32,y:"NEET 2023 \xB7 7 May",tp:"System of Particles and Rotational Motion",st:"Angular acceleration direction",q:"The angular acceleration of a body, moving along the circumference of a circle, is:",o:["along the axis of rotation","along the radius, away from centre","along the radius towards the centre","along the tangent to its position"],c:0,s:"Angular acceleration of a body moving on a circle points along the axis of rotation."},{id:"neet-2023-q034",pn:34,y:"NEET 2023 \xB7 7 May",tp:"System of Particles and Rotational Motion",st:"Radius of gyration",q:"The ratio of radius of gyration of a solid sphere of mass M and radius R about its own axis to the radius of gyration of the thin hollow sphere of same mass and radius about its axis is :",o:["5 : 2","3 : 5","5 : 3","2 : 5"],a:"2*",s:`Radius of gyration of a solid sphere about its axis is \u221A(2/5) R.
For a thin hollow sphere it is \u221A(2/3) R.
Ratio = \u221A(2/5) \xD7 \u221A(3/2) = \u221A(3/5).
Note: the paper says none of the options is correct; the true ratio is \u221A(3/5).
Note: printed answer 2 (marked 2*); the paper itself says none of the options is correct and the exact ratio is \u221A(3/5), not 3 : 5.`},{id:"neet-2022r-q001",pn:1,y:"Re-NEET 2022 \xB7 4 Sep",tp:"System of Particles and Rotational Motion",st:"Rotational kinetic energy",q:"An energy of 484 J is spent in increasing the speed of a flywheel from 60 rpm to 360 rpm. The moment of inertia of the flywheel is",o:["0.07 kg-m\xB2","0.7 kg-m\xB2","3.22 kg-m\xB2","30.8 kg-m\xB2"],c:1,s:`Convert to rad/s: \u03C9\u1D62 = 60 \xD7 (2\u03C0)/(60) = 2\u03C0 rad/s and \u03C9f = 360 \xD7 (2\u03C0)/(60) = 12\u03C0 rad/s.
Energy spent = change in rotational kinetic energy = (1/2)I(\u03C9f\xB2 \u2212 \u03C9\u1D62\xB2).
So 484 = (1/2) \xD7 I \xD7 [(12\u03C0)\xB2 \u2212 (2\u03C0)\xB2] = (1/2) \xD7 I \xD7 140\u03C0\xB2.
I = (2 \xD7 484)/(140\u03C0\xB2) \u2248 0.7 kg-m\xB2.
Note: the printed working uses 486, which gives the same rounded answer.`},{id:"neet-2022-q012",pn:12,y:"NEET 2022 \xB7 17 Jul",tp:"System of Particles and Rotational Motion",st:"Radius of gyration of disc",q:"The ratio of the radius of gyration of a thin uniform disc about an axis passing through its centre and normal to its plane to the radius of gyration of the disc about its diameter is",o:["\u221A2 : 1","4 : 1","1 : \u221A2","2 : 1"],c:0,s:`About the axis normal to the plane: I\u2081 = MR\xB2/2, so k\u2081 = \u221A(I\u2081/M) = R/\u221A2.
About a diameter: I\u2082 = MR\xB2/4, so k\u2082 = R/2.
k\u2081/k\u2082 = (R/\u221A2)/(R/2) = \u221A2.
Ratio = \u221A2 : 1.`,sfig:{w:180,h:100,alt:"Two discs: the first with the axis perpendicular to the plane through the centre (cross in a circle, curved arrow), the second with the axis along a vertical diameter (curved arrow at its top end).",d:[["c",45,50,30],["c",45,50,6],["l",41,46,49,54],["l",49,46,41,54],["p","M57.1 57.0A14 14 0 1 1 52.0 37.9"],["p","M54.6 39.4L48.1 38.6L50.7 34.1z","F"],["c",135,50,30],["l",135,6,135,94],["p","M128.4 9.6A7 7 0 0 1 141.6 9.6"],["p","M142.6 12.4L138.1 7.7L143.0 5.9z","F"]]}},{id:"neet-2022r-q012",pn:12,y:"Re-NEET 2022 \xB7 4 Sep",tp:"System of Particles and Rotational Motion",st:"Centre of mass motion",q:`Given below are two statements: one is labelled as Assertion (A) and the other is labelled as Reason (R).
Assertion (A): When a fire cracker (rocket) explodes in mid air, its fragments fly in such a way that they continue moving in the same path, which the fire cracker would have followed, had it not exploded.
Reason (R): Explosion of cracker (rocket) occurs due to Internal forces only and no external force acts for this explosion.
In the light of the above statements, choose the most appropriate answer from the options given below`,o:["(A) is not correct but (R) is correct","Both (A) and (R) are correct and (R) is the correct explanation of (A)","both (A) and (R) are correct but (R) is not the correct explanation of (A)","(A) is correct but (R) is not correct"],c:0,s:`Only the centre of mass of the rocket continues on the original path, not the individual fragments.
So (A) is wrong.
(R) is correct: the explosion is due to internal forces only, so the motion of the centre of mass is unchanged.`},{id:"neet-2022-q018",pn:18,y:"NEET 2022 \xB7 17 Jul",tp:"System of Particles and Rotational Motion",st:"Explosion and momentum conservation",q:"A shell of mass m is at rest initially. It explodes into three fragments having mass in the ratio 2 : 2 : 1. If the fragments having equal mass fly off along mutually perpendicular directions with speed v, the speed of the third (lighter) fragment is",o:["\u221A2 v","2\u221A2 v","3\u221A2 v","v"],c:1,s:`Momentum is conserved and the initial momentum is zero, so the final momentum is zero.
Take masses 2m, 2m and m.
The two big fragments give 2mv along x and 2mv along y.
Their resultant is \u221A((2mv)\xB2 + (2mv)\xB2) = 2\u221A2 mv.
The light fragment must cancel it: m v\u2032 = 2\u221A2 mv.
So v\u2032 = 2\u221A2 v.`},{id:"neet-2022-q021",pn:21,y:"NEET 2022 \xB7 17 Jul",tp:"System of Particles and Rotational Motion",st:"Angular acceleration",q:"The angular speed of a fly wheel moving with uniform angular acceleration changes from 1200 rpm to 3120 rpm in 16 seconds. The angular acceleration in rad/s\xB2 is",o:["4\u03C0","12\u03C0","104\u03C0","2\u03C0"],c:0,s:`\u03B1 = (\u03C9_f \u2013 \u03C9\u1D62)/t.
Convert rpm to rad/s by multiplying by 2\u03C0/60.
\u03B1 = ((3120 \u2013 1200)/16) \xD7 (2\u03C0/60).
\u03B1 = 120 \xD7 2\u03C0/60 = 4\u03C0 rad/s\xB2.`},{id:"neet-2022-q030",pn:30,y:"NEET 2022 \xB7 17 Jul",tp:"System of Particles and Rotational Motion",st:"Centre of mass of two particles",q:"Two objects of mass 10 kg and 20 kg respectively are connected to the two ends of a rigid rod of length 10 m with negligible mass. The distance of the center of mass of the system from the 10 kg mass is",o:["20/3 m","10 m","5 m","10/3 m"],c:0,s:`Take the 10 kg mass at x = 0 and the 20 kg mass at x = 10 m.
X_cm = (m\u2081x\u2081 + m\u2082x\u2082)/(m\u2081 + m\u2082) = (10 \xD7 0 + 20 \xD7 10)/(10 + 20).
X_cm = 200/30 = 20/3 m from the 10 kg mass.`,sfig:{w:230,h:130,alt:"x-y axes with the 10 kg mass at (0, 0) and the 20 kg mass at (10, 0) on the x-axis, joined by the rod.",d:[["l",20,90,200,90,"a"],["l",50,115,50,10,"a"],["t",206,94,"x"],["t",44,14,"y","e"],["c",50,90,3.5,"F"],["c",140,90,3.5,"F"],["t",56,70,"(0, 0)"],["t",120,70,"(10, 0)"],["t",56,108,"10 kg"],["t",120,108,"20 kg"]]}}]})();
